Introduction
It is common for software applications to rely on external files for instructions, settings, and other configuration parameters rather than hardcoding every value directly into the program. Separating configuration from application logic makes software easier to customize, maintain, and deploy, while also allowing users to modify its behavior without changing or recompiling the underlying source code.
Configuration files serve many purposes; they can store application settings, user preferences, connection details, paths to external resources, feature flags, and other parameters that may change between different environments or use cases. This separation is particularly useful when the same program needs to operate with different configurations without requiring changes to the program itself.
Throughout various programming languages, there are many formats and approaches for storing configuration data, ranging from simple key-value files to more structured formats such as JSON, YAML, and TOML. One of the traditional and relatively simple formats is the INI-style configuration file, which organizes settings into sections containing key-value pairs.
When it comes to the MQL5 programming language, configurations can be passed to the program using the program's inputs, which can be saved and loaded from a.SET file. The Python programming language, on the other hand, provides the
configparser
module as part of its standard library, for working with INI-style configuration files. It provides a convenient interface for reading, modifying, and writing configuration data while supporting features such as sections, default values, interpolation, and type conversion.
In the previous article, we built the notorious matplotlib framework
.  In this one, we will explore the Python-configparser module and develop a similar configuration interface for MQL5. The goal is to mimic Python's implementation and provide a Python-like MQL5-friendly API that brings the same general concepts and convenience to MQL5 applications.
What is the configparser Module?
According to the documentation:
This module provides the
ConfigParser
class that implements a basic configuration language, which provides a structure similar to what’s found in Microsoft Windows INI files. You can use this to write Python programs that can be customized by end users easily.
To understand what the module does, we have to understand the structure of a so-called INI file.
Understanding INI Files
An INI file is a configuration file for computer software that consists of plain text with a structure and syntax comprising key–value pairs
o
rganized in sections.
The name of these configuration files comes from the filename extension INI, short for initialization, used in the
MS-DOS
operating system, which popularized this method of software configuration. The format has become an informal standard in many contexts of configuration, but many applications on other operating systems use different filename extensions, such as conf and cfg.
Example.ini
; last modified 1 April 2001 by John Doe
[account]
login = 12345678
broker = SomeBroker
currency = USD

[strategy]
name = Moving Average Crossover
timeframe = H1
symbol = EURUSD
01: Sections
Key-value pairs inside the INI file may be grouped under a section. Some INI dialects require every key-value pair to be in a section; some allow so-called global properties. When key-value pairs are grouped, the section name appears on a line by itself, enclosed in square brackets ( [ and ] ), and applies to all key-value pairs on subsequent lines until another section is declared.
There is no explicit "end of section" delimiter (e.g. XML's </tag>). Thus, sections syntactically cannot be arbitrarily nested. When required, nesting can be implemented through flattening one's hierarchy and concatenating with a custom delimiter character inside the section name (often.).  One level of nesting is often supported, called
subsections
.
Example INI document employing nested subsections:
[account]
login = 12345678
broker = SomeBroker
currency = USD

[
strategy
]
name = Moving Average Crossover
timeframe = H1
symbol = EURUSD

[
strategy
.
risk
]
max_risk = 2.0
max_daily_loss = 5.0
max_positions = 10

[
strategy
.
indicators
]
fast_period = 20
slow_period = 50
02: Case sensitivity
Section and property names in Windows are case-insensitive.
03: Comments
Comments can be used in an INI configuration file to document settings or temporarily disable configuration options. A comment begins with a semicolon (;) or, in some INI dialects, a number sign (#).
A line beginning with either of these characters is treated as a comment and is therefore ignored when the configuration is parsed.
# This is a comment
; This is also a comment

# var = a
; var = b

var = a
foo = bar
However, the use of inline comments should be avoided. Although some INI implementations support comments appearing after a value, this behavior is not consistent across all INI parsers and can cause the comment text to be interpreted as part of the value.
For example:
var = a
; This is an inline comment
foo = bar
# This is another inline comment
04: Order of section and properties
The order of properties in a section and the order of sections in a file is irrelevant.
05: Duplicate names
Most implementations only support having one property with a given name in a section. The second occurrence of a property name may cause an abort; it may be ignored (and the value discarded), or it may override the first occurrence (with the first value discarded). Some programs use duplicate property names to implement multi-valued properties.
In some implementations, duplicate sections simply merge their properties, as if they occurred contiguously.
06: Quoted values
Some implementations allow values to be quoted, typically using double quotes and/or apostrophes ( ' ). This allows for explicit declaration of whitespace and/or for quoting of special characters (equals, semicolon, etc.).
