Note that configparser module is not limited to files with the
.ini
extension. It can also work with other configuration files, such as.cfg and.conf. The file extension itself does not determine whether a file can be parsed; what matters is whether its contents follow the INI-style configuration format expected by configparser.
Obtaining Values From a Configuration File
In Python, configparser provides a straightforward way to access values stored in a configuration file. Two common approaches are
indexing
and using the get() method.
01: Using section and option names as indexes
cfg = configparser.ConfigParser(inline_comment_prefixes=
None
)
cfg.read(
"example.ini"
)
print
(cfg[
"account"
][
"login"
])
02: Using the get() method
print
(cfg.get(
"account"
,
"login"
))
Both approaches retrieve the same value:
(.env) C:\>python main.py
